From: Diogo Pascoal <dmtpascoal@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: unregister device notifiers when freeing netdevs

mtk_add_mac() registers a netdevice notifier for each MAC on QDMA SoCs,
but only mtk_unreg_dev() removed it. Several probe error paths (e.g. a
failed dummy NAPI allocation, err_free_dev, err_deinit_ppe) free the
netdevs without going through mtk_unreg_dev(), leaving a freed notifier on
the netdevice chain, used after free on the next netdevice event.

Unregister the notifiers in mtk_free_dev() too. Unregistering one that is
not on the chain only returns -ENOENT, so the normal remove path, which
calls mtk_unreg_dev() first, is unaffected.

Not yet verified on hardware.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index 226769b..01c5753 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -4865,8 +4865,20 @@ static int mtk_free_dev(struct mtk_eth *eth)
 	int i;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < MTK_MAX_DEVS; i++) {
+		struct mtk_mac *mac;
+
 		if (!eth->netdev[i])
 			continue;
+
+		/* mtk_add_mac() registers the notifier as soon as the netdev
+		 * exists, but only mtk_unreg_dev() removed it, and several
+		 * probe error paths free the netdevs without going through
+		 * mtk_unreg_dev(). Unregistering one that is not on the chain
+		 * just returns -ENOENT.
+		 */
+		mac = netdev_priv(eth->netdev[i]);
+		if (MTK_HAS_CAPS(eth->soc->caps, MTK_QDMA))
+			unregister_netdevice_notifier(&mac->device_notifier);
 		free_netdev(eth->netdev[i]);
 	}
 
